<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zaki Khan Zand</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1779">1779</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zaki Khan Zand was a half-brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan Zand</a>, known for his cruelty. On Karim Khan's death in 1779 he seized power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> in the name of Karim Khan's young sons and had rivals killed, but his brutality turned his own troops against him, and he was murdered within months, opening the Zand civil wars.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zaki Khan was born into the Zand tribe, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> had moved from its lands near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a> to Northern Khorasan. After Nader's death in 1747 the Zand returned to Lorestan, and their leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> in 1750 and ruled most of central and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> under the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakil">vakil</a>, deputy. Zaki Khan was doubly related to him, a first cousin through their fathers and a half-brother through their mother, and his fortunes followed those of his protector, whose leading general he became.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Iranian sources describe him as already notorious for cruelty and opportunism. In 1763, returning from the last campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azad-khan-afghan">Azad Khan Afghan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> and apparently feeling unrewarded, he seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> and exploited its people mercilessly. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan</a> marched against him he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dezful">Dezful</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzestan</a>, recruited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhtiari">Bakhtiari</a> warriors and allied with the rebellious local governor, but after a year he begged for his brother's mercy and was pardoned. In 1773 a naval expedition he led against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> ended in failure and disgrace. Two years later he led a punitive expedition against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> stronghold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mazandaran">Mazandaran</a>, where he succeeded and left a reputation for brutal terror.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan</a> died on 2 March 1779 after a long illness. Zaki Khan, whose daughter was married to Karim's young second son Mohammad-Ali Khan, had most supporters of the elder son, Abol-Fath Khan, killed and Abol-Fath imprisoned; Mohammad Ali was proclaimed ruler with Zaki Khan as regent. Under pressure from the boys' mother, he accepted that both sons would be invested. His rule was contested at once: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> hostage Agha Mohammad Khan escaped, Zaki Khan's nephew Ali-Morad Khan mutinied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadeq-khan-zand">Sadeq Khan Zand</a> raised an army in the south-east.</p><p>Marching on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, Zaki Khan committed atrocities at Izadkhvast that shocked even his own men, and a group of tribal leaders murdered him in his tent there on 6 June 1779. The struggle for the throne continued between Sadeq Khan and Ali-Morad Khan. His youngest son, Akbar Khan Zand, was blinded and killed in 1782 by his cousin, the future shah Jafar Khan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zaki Khan Zand” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His half-brother <b>Zaki Khan</b> seized power in the name of Karim Khan's sons Abu al-Fath Khan and Mohammad Ali Khan but was murdered within months.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zand-empire">Zand Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Malayer, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in Izadkhast, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.29,48.82&amp;year=1769" aria-label="Malayer on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Zaki Khan Zand"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4215 4595h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4215 4595h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.29,48.82&amp;year=1769">What was here in 1769? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
